2014-09-30 17:28 — Janis Papanagnou:
> On 30.09.2014 16:36, Benjamin A. Schmit wrote:
>> 2014-09-30 16:06 — Janis Papanagnou:
>>> I'm interested in practical experience with the convict patch.
>>> [...]
>> [...]  I'd especially recommend my article
>> “Convict 0.7 - Experiences and two bugs” from April 27th, 2012.
> Thanks. (I'll reply to it here to avoid using the google interface...)
> 
>> Survived the early game.  (With the new adjustments, it is now much
>> easier to avoid starving early, i.e. less dependent on RNG luck.
> What adjustments are you referring to?
I think I was referring to changes between 0.6 and 0.7 — here's the
original release note on that point:

2012-04-18 21:13 — Karl Garrisson:
>  * Slightly more forgiving early game: no more luck penalty, and the
> convict loses nutrition at half the normal rate, but only when he
> reaches "hungry" or worse.  The starting nutrition and negative
> starting alignment are unchanged, however.

>> Caution and prayers got me through the first three floors, [...]
> Aha. (I somehow thought that prayer would not be possible with convicts
> in the early game; due to -1 luck, or so, I had in mind.)
It is, after a while (alignment is negative, so immediate prayer doesn't
work for sure).  Figuring out when it is and how to balance waiting with
fainting and the possible bad effects of an early prayer was important,
IIRC.

>> The first and second mines levels were lit – that got me the armor I so
>> desperately needed.  The second one had a magic lamp too, which I
>> promptly converted to Mjollnir, dropping the iron ball.
> Being able to drop the ball by prayer, I suppose?
Not sure, might also have been remove curse, wand of opening, or a
number of other possibilities.  Or by digging a pit, dropping the ball
in it and filling it up.  Digging again gives you the ball without the
chain.

>> [...] Mine town had a second magic lamp, which I used as a permanent
>> light source from that point on (a good thing for humans in general).
> Well, two early magic lamps; you've been very lucky. :-)
Definitely, but the first is the one that really made the difference.

>> After I saw that there were exactly 7 candles, I left the rest of the
>> mines for later, but somehow never got to it.
> You acquired the luckstone elsewhere, I suppose?
Probably not.  I'm not usually hunting after it if something else seems
more important at the moment, and with Luck Blade I sure didn't need to
go after it any more.

>> I descended the main dungeon cautiously.  A number of chaotic altars
>> gave me (at different points) Luck Blade [...]
> (Aha, so this artifact is inherent part of the convict patch.)
> 
>> Some general points on the convict:
>> [...]
>> Due to the high number of co-aligned altars (4 in total), [...]
> Available co-aligned altars is crucial with that role?
At least one is definitely a help, but I think I also managed to do
without them.  Not that role-specific, really…

>> [...]  The tourist can proceed slowly and retreat often, while
>> the Convict is driven down by hunger. [...]
> I see; a significant factor that makes the role hard.
Exactly; that was my main point.  Hard but also more interesting, I'd say.

>> Even with Mjollnir, the HPs still didn't make it possible to play the
>> convict warrior-style – retreat and use of the stack of (skilled)
>> daggers is important as well.  Generally, I find the patch well-balanced
>> now.
> An interesting report. Thanks again.
You're welcome!


